  "name": "Question 9",
  "problem": "The weights of apples in a certain orchard are normally distributed with a mean of $150\\,g$ and a standard deviation of $5\\,g.$ What is the probability that a randomly selected apple weighs more than $158\\,g?$ **Round your answer to two decimal places.**\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.*\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.00\\,$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,1.5\\,$ | $\\,0.9332\\,$ | $\\,0.9345\\,$ | $\\,0.9357\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.6\\,$ | $\\,0.9452\\,$ | $\\,0.9463\\,$ | $\\,0.9474\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.7\\,$ | $\\,0.9554\\,$ | $\\,0.9564\\,$ | $\\,0.9573\\,$ |\n\nThe probability that a randomly selected apple weighs more than $158\\,g$ is approximately\n{{field-1}}.",
